===Etymology 1===
{{PIE root|en|h₁wers}}
From {{der|en|la|Ūrania||muse of astronomy in Greek mythology}} {{suffix|en||an|pos2=''suffix forming {{glossary|agent noun}}s''}}. ''Ūrania'' is derived from {{der|en|grc|Οὐρᾰνῐ́ᾱ||muse of astronomy}}, from {{m|grc|οὐράνιος||of or relating to the sky, celestial, heavenly}} (from {{m|grc|οὐρανός||the sky; heaven, home of the gods; the universe}}, probably ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁wers-||rain}}) + {{m|grc|-ιος|pos=''suffix forming adjectives meaning ‘pertaining to’''}}.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=''adj.{{sup|1}}'' and ''n.{{sup|2}}''|noformat=yes|id=220339|date=March 2012|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=Uranian{{sup|1}}|pos=adj.'' and ''n.}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=Urania|pos=n|id=220338|date=March 2012}}</ref>

Adjective sense 5 (“homosexual”) and the noun sense (“a homosexual”) refer to {{w|Plato}}’s work ''[[w:Symposium (Plato)|Symposium]]'' ({{circa2|385–370 {{B.C.E.|dots=on}}|short=yes}}), where the [[goddess]] [[Aphrodite]], in her [[heavenly]] [[aspect]] {{w|Aphrodite Urania}} ''(see adjective sense 2)'' is described as inspiring a [[noble#Adjective|noble]] form of affection between older and younger men.<ref name="OED"/> Compare {{cog|de|Urning||a homosexual, Uranian}}, {{m|de|Urnigtum||homosexuality}}, also referring to Aphrodite Urania, coined by the German writer {{w|Karl Heinrich Ulrichs}} (1825–1895) in 1864. By the 1900s, the use of the word in this sense had largely been supplanted by {{m|en|homosexual}} ''(see that entry for more)''.

