#: {{antonyms|en|pandemian|pandemic}}
# {{lb|en|Greek mythology}} Relating to [[Urania]], the [[muse#Noun|muse]] of [[astronomy]].
# {{lb|en|by extension|historical|rare}} Of or pertaining to astronomy; [[astronomical]].
#: {{synonyms|en|uranical|q1=archaic, rare}}
# {{lb|en|literary|archaic}} [[homosexual#Adjective|Homosexual]]; {{lb|en|specifically}} relating to a [[man#Noun|man]]'s [[erotic]] love for [[adolescent#Adjective|adolescent]] [[boy]]s; [[pederastic]]; and {{lb|en|poetic}} [[convey]]ing [[appreciation]] for [[young#Adjective|young]] men.
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:homosexual}}
#* '''1983''', Eric Garber and Lyn Paleo, ''Uranian worlds: a reader's guide to alternative sexuality in science fiction and fantasy''
#*: {{quote|en|The title of this bibliography is derived from the nineteenth- century word for homosexual — '''Uranian'''. The word was coined by the early German homosexual emancipationist, Karl Ulrichs, and was popularly used through the First World War.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Linda Dowling|title=Hellenism and Homosexuality in Victorian Oxford|page=134|passage=Before these ideals would once again become available to "homosexuality" as a positive social identity, however, the '''Uranian''' modality of male love would be rejected by the avant-garde itself as an outworn fashion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Chuck Stewart|title=Gay and Lesbian Issues: A Reference Handbook|page=138|passage=Carpenter was disappointed by these meetings with Whitman but began to integrate a positive homosexual view into his socialist writings. Carpenter saw the '''Uranian''' (homosexual) spirit to be more enlightened than that of the common man and believed that Uranians were the new prophets of the coming social revolution.}}

